(VAR-03, VAR-04, VAR-06: `Set a TYPE called NAME to VALUE.` and `Create a
 TYPE called NAME to VALUE.` both declare and initialize, and are the same
 form as `a TYPE called NAME is VALUE.` with a different lead-in word. The
 lead-in and the connector vary independently: all five spellings below
 compile and produce the same kind of variable. `to` after a bare `a`
 lead-in (`a number called n to 9.`) is the one combination that is
 rejected — see Discrepancy 7.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox VAR-03.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   1
   2
   3
   4
   5
)
Set a number called one is 1.
Create a number called two is 2.
Set a number called three to 3.
Create a number called four to 4.
a number called five is 5.
Print one.
Print two.
Print three.
Print four.
Print five.
